<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ryotwari">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ryotwari</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ryotwari system</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ryotwari system was a land revenue system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, associated with Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-munro">Thomas Munro</a>, under which the colonial government collected revenue directly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">ryots</a>, the individual cultivators, rather than through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> described it in 1857, each registered holder was recognised as proprietor, could sublet, sell or mortgage his land, and could not be ejected while he paid a fixed money assessment. Used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> Presidency, where Munro became governor in 1820, it was extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>. Munro cut the demand from a half to a third of the gross produce, and payment in cash left cultivators at the mercy of moneylenders when crops failed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ryotwari.jpg" alt="Ryotwari" width="330" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Archer Shee · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Thomas_Munro,_1st_Bt_by_Sir_Martin_Archer_Shee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
